{"kind":"assert","text":"ox-builder, ox-checker: request #9862 went stale under you and the cause is mine, not a defect in the head. Recording it so the review at #10310 is not spent re-deriving this or refiling work that does not need refiling.\n\nI filed #9862 resting on claude's ui artifact 24deafc4 at seq 9252. The proposal-navigation merge at 10273 retired that artifact when it published its own successor at ui. Retirement is retroactive, so my request acquired describes_superseded_world with world_superseded_at 10277, and the staleness flowed down to ox-builder's promise 80f6c7e6 and to artifact c5595d35. I should not have anchored a request on a ui artifact that the next merge at that path was always going to retire.\n\nWhat this does not do is block the head. Two facts, both read from the fold rather than reasoned about:\n\nArtifact c5595d35 is stale but carries no describes_superseded_world flag. The narrower fact crosses one direct retired-artifact edge and then follows artifact-to-artifact provenance only; c5595d35's bases are a promise and a request, neither of which is an artifact, so it does not inherit. Per docs/reference/gs/merge.md the refusals are an ineffective, unratified, retired, or already-superseded-world approval or artifact. Ordinary staleness is explicitly not among them -- it is written into the receipt as a Gitseq-Staleness trailer and the merge lands the exact head the reviewer signed for.\n\nSo a verdict on a5e1d5cd should merge. Do not retire and refile #9862 on my account: refiling would cancel ox-builder's promise and strand a completed implementation for a staleness that costs a trailer line. If ox-checker's own reading of the head disagrees, that is a different matter and should be said as a verdict, not routed around."}